WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.210
- Edgar Allan Poe, or
as he was known in life,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.000
just Edgar Poe.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.340
We remember him for his

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.630
spine tingling, tales of
horror and the macabre,

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.750
as well as inventing the
detective fiction genre.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.600
But unlike many of the great authors

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.920
of Western classic literature,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.650
he's become an icon unto himself,

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.880
recognized to this day by name,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.630
arguably as much as the titles
of his stories and poems.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:29.670
Aside from the odd raven or black cat,

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.720
none of this stuff features any
characters from his stories.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.960
It's the cult of personality
of the man himself

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.577
that has stuck most in popular
consciousness, but why?

00:00:41.464 --> 00:00:44.047
(upbeat music)

00:00:45.694 --> 00:00:49.100
Poe lived in many places
during his short 40 years

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.870
on this cursed planet,

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.840
and he is claimed as a
famous historical resident

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.840
of Boston, Richmond, Baltimore,
New York, Sullivan's Island,

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.340
Philadelphia, London, and even Scotland.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.860
So there is this feeling of
collective ownership for many.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.630
Baltimore even named their
football team the Ravens

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.080
after Poe's poem, and maybe that's why

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.560
their Super Bowl
championships are nevermore.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.360
It's no secret that Poe had a
difficult and tormented life,

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.460
a starving artist who
could never catch a break,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.510
making a pittance on his writing,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.200
no matter how popular it was.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.180
And you know, people vibe with a sad boy,

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.790
look at Jack Skellington,
and just look at him.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.460
But was he really that sad?

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.180
Like this picture, for instance,

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.750
of all the many portraits of him

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.440
that were taken during his life,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.080
the one taken right
after his suicide attempt

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.280
has become the most ubiquitous.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.140
It is unquestionably the worst
photo of him that exists.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.920
After Poe died, from what
many people believe now

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.430
was a brain tumor,

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.450
this was the image that
was pushed into the public

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.570
by his literary enemies.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.910
Seriously, this guy had a
list of nemeses a mile long.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.830
It's basically like the
press using his mugshot

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:08.470
to make him look bad.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.500
His foster father, John
Allan, nemesis number one,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.000
bought the suit in the photo

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.840
for Poe's beloved foster mother's funeral.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.460
John Allan hated Edgar

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.660
and never spent money on
him if he could help it.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.700
But as a high society gentlemen,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.170
it would've been embarrassing

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.560
for his foster son not
to wear mourning clothes.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.280
He wasn't goth,

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.413
He was just poor.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.000
John Allan adopted Poe
when he was orphaned

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.450
by his stage actor
parents at the age of two.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.190
Allan was one of the
wealthiest men in Richmond

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.030
and raised Poe to be a society gentleman

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.320
who was expected to
live a certain lifestyle

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.900
or else shame the whole family.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.630
But John Allan also refused to

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.240
adequately support Poe financially

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.730
repeatedly setting him up for failure.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.660
He sent Poe off to university

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.380
without enough money
to pay for his classes,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.860
so Poe had to drop out.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.300
When Poe joined the army
to follow in the footsteps

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.470
of his renowned military grandfather,

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.990
Allan helped Poe enroll at West Point,

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.950
but did not provide Edgar

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.040
with the means to actually
acquire the education,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.100
so Poe was dishonorably discharged.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.530
But why would the man who
raised him hate him so much?

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.520
Well, when Poe and his
foster mother Frances

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.390
found out that John Allan was keeping two,

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:34.110
count them, two secret separate families,

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.030
Poe took Frances Allan's side

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.990
'cause he was a hashtag feminist,

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:40.210
so daddy cut him off
and went out of his way

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.570
to make Edgar's life miserable,

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.470
disowning and disinheriting him.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:49.120
Says Harry Lee Poe, former
president of the Poe foundation

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.297
and a family relative,

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:54.047
"Poe never signed his
name Edgar Allan Poe,

00:03:54.047 --> 00:03:56.287
"nor did any of his
writings in his lifetime

00:03:56.287 --> 00:03:57.787
"appear with this name.

00:03:57.787 --> 00:04:01.880
"He used the initial A but
never the name of John Allan."

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.210
The reason we remember
him as Edgar Allan Poe now

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:10.070
is thanks to his greatest
nemesis of all, Rufus Griswold.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.820
These two men had once
been amicable colleagues

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.500
but after Poe gave Griswold's
writings a lukewarm review

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.850
in a literary magazine,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.690
Griswold had an out for Poe
for the rest of eternity.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:22.880
As soon as Poe died,

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.140
Griswold released a
scathingly, slanderous obituary

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:31.200
depicting Poe as a raving
madman doomed to hell,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.210
which is a lot. (claps hands)

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.660
As Kent Ljungquist put it

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.207
in the "Cambridge Companion
to Edgar Allan Poe,"

00:04:37.207 --> 00:04:40.017
"The obituary was a
combination of half truths

00:04:40.017 --> 00:04:41.457
"and outright fabrications

00:04:41.457 --> 00:04:44.527
"about Poe's personal habits and conduct.

00:04:44.527 --> 00:04:47.187
"Griswold's portrait in
which Poe's role as a critic

00:04:47.187 --> 00:04:51.037
"was relegated to that of
a 'dissector of sentences',

00:04:51.037 --> 00:04:54.310
"almost irrevocably
damaged his reputation."

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.330
Griswold also con Poe's mother-in-law

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.730
into turning over all
of Poe's writing to him.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:02.970
He published Poe's collected works

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.510
with an introduction that
slandered him terribly,

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.260
and then kept all the money for himself.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.130
Poe's family never saw a dime.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.537
To, again, quote Harry Lee Poe,

00:05:14.537 --> 00:05:16.457
"Griswold stated that Poe's stories

00:05:16.457 --> 00:05:18.857
"were largely autobiographical.

00:05:18.857 --> 00:05:21.237
"While many of Poe's love poems

00:05:21.237 --> 00:05:24.197
"were clearly inspired by
events in his own life,

00:05:24.197 --> 00:05:28.097
"Griswold suggested that
Poe's horror and terror pieces

00:05:28.097 --> 00:05:29.537
"were as well.

00:05:29.537 --> 00:05:32.017
"The tradition of
interpreting Poe's stories

00:05:32.017 --> 00:05:34.667
"as autobiographical in their details

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:38.520
"continues among critics
and scholars to this day."

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.770
When in life, Poe said the opposite,

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.100
that he didn't attempt

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.330
to express his own self for the stories

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.880
but that instead he focused
on only creating beauty.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:48.850
But thanks to Griswold

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.710
and the slander of Poe's
other many literary enemies,

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.530
we remember him as this frumpy, sad boy

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.500
who saddened himself to death.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.740
But, again, why?

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.580
You see, Poe worked as a literary critic.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.790
See, at the time it was
common practice for critics

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:09.660
to only print favorable
reviews of their friends' books

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.520
in exchange for puff pieces
of their own works in return.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.400
Poe wasn't playing that.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.890
In his career with various
literary magazines,

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:22.010
he pursued the practice of
balanced, honest criticism,

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:25.860
focusing on both positives
and negatives in the stories.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.780
Poe often looked down
on his contemporaries

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.970
for their tendency towards imitation.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:35.020
Poe considered himself
a genius of originality.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.170
To quote another famous literary snob,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.237
and by snob I mean icon Oscar Wilde,

00:06:40.237 --> 00:06:43.947
"imitation is the
sincerest form of flattery

00:06:43.947 --> 00:06:46.347
"that mediocrity can pay to greatness."

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.760
Says scholar of 19th century
literature, Sandra Tomc,

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:53.807
in the "Cambridge Companion
to Edgar Allan Poe,"

00:06:53.807 --> 00:06:57.083
"As a critic he could
not and would not lie.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:00.557
"As a consequence, he made enemies,

00:07:00.557 --> 00:07:04.420
"but their number was
legion, and he was only one."

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:05.620
But while Poe was alive,

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.950
he was as much known for his
tales of humor and grand hoaxes

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:11.900
as his tales of terror.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:15.440
Known as his burlesques,
these comedic stories

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.070
include elements of satire and wit

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.410
that often resembles slapstick.

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:23.120
Poe found humor in everyday situations,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.620
especially among people who
took themselves too seriously.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:29.240
And yeah, a lot of the times

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.580
these were thinly veiled
mockery of his literary peers

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.340
who retaliated by painting him publicly

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.550
as a friendless, lunatic.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.650
Says Robert T. Tally Jr.,

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:44.417
professor of English at Texas
State University, go Texas,

00:07:44.417 --> 00:07:47.927
"One could make the case that
Poe was primarily a humorist,

00:07:47.927 --> 00:07:50.297
"if sometimes a black humorist,

00:07:50.297 --> 00:07:52.627
"and that his tales of terror or mystery

00:07:52.627 --> 00:07:56.930
"were secondary to the main
body of his collected works."

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.837
But as Lee Poe says,

00:07:58.837 --> 00:08:02.207
"His humor does not survive
today because it's usually

00:08:02.207 --> 00:08:04.667
"dependent upon the reader's familiarity

00:08:04.667 --> 00:08:08.267
"with a timely situation
he would satirize."

00:08:08.267 --> 00:08:09.727
"Poe probably suffers from this

00:08:09.727 --> 00:08:11.927
"misconception of his body of work

00:08:11.927 --> 00:08:14.177
"more than any other American writer

00:08:14.177 --> 00:08:16.677
"because the middle school and high school

00:08:16.677 --> 00:08:18.597
"American literature anthologies

00:08:18.597 --> 00:08:21.577
"tend to select a few of
his most gruesome stories

00:08:21.577 --> 00:08:23.870
"for their young audience."

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.120
These gruesome stories include

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.960
his famous tales of the
Arabesque and the Grotesque.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.890
Poe elevated the horror
genre changing it from

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.080
the common penny dreadful blood and gore

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.760
to something deeper.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.440
His stories and poetry explore themes of

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.110
the alienation of loss,

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.270
the question of life after
death and the existence of God,

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.260
the power of love over death,

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.450
the human race's capacity
for evil and cruelty,

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:52.400
the irrationality and
irony of vanity, pride,

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.910
jealousy, guilt, revenge,

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.130
both legal justice

00:08:56.130 --> 00:09:00.100
and a universal, cosmic,
metaphysical justice,

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.240
as well as science is both a danger

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.470
and a guide to spiritual truth.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.910
Poe was also a pioneer of science fiction,

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:10.220
inspiring the likes of H.
G. Wells, and Jules Verne

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:12.800
and many other giants of the genre.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:14.190
Not as many as Mary Shelley,

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:16.890
but we can give him a
credit where credit is due.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:18.650
He wrote stories of air travel

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.332
across the Atlantic and to the moon,

00:09:21.332 --> 00:09:22.740
of dystopian futures

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:25.910
and of the exploration of the
line between life and death,

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:29.100
adventures beyond the
boundaries of the known world,

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.100
and the sentience of matter

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:34.550
and the relativity of time and space.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:37.370
When acknowledging his own
debt to Poe's influence,

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.277
Arthur Ray Bradbury said,

00:09:39.277 --> 00:09:41.387
"Poe's influence on the modern short story

00:09:41.387 --> 00:09:44.537
"is unmatched in English
and American literature.

00:09:44.537 --> 00:09:46.777
"He gave it style, organization, dignity,

00:09:46.777 --> 00:09:49.057
"and meaning and set it on the road

00:09:49.057 --> 00:09:52.227
"to becoming one of the most
challenging forms of fiction

00:09:52.227 --> 00:09:53.970
"a writer can attempt."

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.500
He gave us a twist on
first person narrative,

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.160
the unreliable narrator.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.620
He upended the concept
of the morality tale.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:05.410
In his day, it was often insisted
all stories have a moral,

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:06.607
but Poe said,

00:10:06.607 --> 00:10:10.710
"Beauty is the sole legitimate
province of the poem."

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:11.970
He criticized his peers

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:15.730
for their insistence in
teaching didactic, moral lessons

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.000
and mocked them in satire.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.700
Poe's extensive work as a literary critic

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.390
also forced him to reflect
on his own writing.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:25.830
He revised many of his most
famous works multiple times

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:27.270
and republished them.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.240
We remember Edgar Allan Poe
the way we do in large part

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.290
because he was the victim of
malicious posthumous branding,

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:36.530
but he was so much more than a sad boy

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:40.300
with a bad, terrible, no good portrait.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:43.560
Of course, his horror stories
and poems were influential

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.610
but he had much more
impact on other genres

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:49.000
in a way that is rarely discussed.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.240
Plus, despite his enemies

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.610
trying to ruin and bury his legacy,

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:54.710
I have never even heard of that Griswold

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:56.840
until I wrote this video script.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:00.410
To quote the Raven, "Haters gonna hate."

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.270
Before you go, we here at "It's Lit"

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:04.220
are launching a podcast

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:07.497
hosted by the amazing
Princess Weekes, that's me.

00:11:07.497 --> 00:11:08.960
"It's Lit [Unabridged]" features

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.730
your favorite literature topics

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:14.920
and thoughtful, funny
conversations with amazing authors.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:18.220
It's basically like joining
the best book club ever

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:20.740
but without actually
having to read anything.

